{"input_text": "Question: Calculate the following sum $$\sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n(n+1)}$$ I Tried decomposing the denominator but still seem to be stuck thanks to the numerator 

Answer: With $\sin x=u$ and then $u=\sqrt{t}$ we see
\begin{align}
\int ^{\pi/2}_{0} \sqrt{\sin {2x}} \cdot  \sin{x} \cdot dx
&=\int_0^1\sqrt{2}u^\frac32(1-u^2)^{-\frac14}du \\
&=\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\int_0^1t^\frac14(1-t)^{-\frac14}du \\
&=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\beta(\dfrac{5}{4},\dfrac{3}{4}) \\
&=\color{blue}{\dfrac{\pi}{4}}
\end{align} 
